  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nTranquil wx conditions and warmer than average temperatures will \npersist today into Tuesday on the southern fringes of broad west-\nnorthwest flow aloft. A substantial pattern change then ushers in a \ncooler and more unsettled pattern late Wednesday through Saturday as \nan upper jet streak dives down into the MS Valley, eventually \ncarving out an unseasonably strong 500 mb height minimum into the \nGreat Lakes and Northeast. A leading, compact upper wave in this \ntransition still looks to send a developing frontal wave southeast \ninto the OH Valley and central Appalachians late Wednesday into \nThursday morning. Dynamic nature to the wave and pacific origins \nlikely allows for an area of more substantial rain coverage, \nthough guidance still diverges regarding the track of the system\ncurrently over the north Pacific. Opted to hold with NBM mid \nchance PoPs for now given the lingering uncertainty. \n\nMoist and chilly cyclonic flow behind the departing low \npressure center will keep clouds and shower chances in the \nforecast then into Thursday afternoon and Friday. This regime \nalso sets up a favorable lake effect response parallel to the \nlong axis of Lake MI later Thursday through as late as Saturday \nmorning into far nw IN/sw MI and ne IL. Progged thermal profiles\nsupport rain as the primary precipitation type, though it does \nseem likely that snowflakes may mix in at times inland. Ridging \nbuilds back Sunday into the first half of next week with a \nreturn to dry wx and warming temps.",
